BTW, with the LCM, no matter how many pseudo-random numbers are
produced, the following ones remain cyclic. The more leftmost bits you
cut out in the observation, the smaller the cycle.

It's not this cyclicity that's spoiling things though (e.g. in the
always lawful valkyries phenomenon). The cycle remains very large even
if you only observe the rightmost bit.

The problem is that nethack is seeding *rand48 functions with a 32bit
value. As I don't fully understand all aspects of LCM I just made a
test: with a 32bit seed, the tuple of all subsequent numbers is chosen
from a set of only 2^n tuples, where n is the number of rightmost bits
you observe.

i.e. in nethack terms: if you look at the result modulo 2, whatever you
seed with, the results of subsequent rn2(2) calls will be one of 4
possible tuples:

(rn(2),rn(2),rn(2),...) = {    (a1, a2, a3, ...)
                          { OR (b1, b2, b3, ...)
                          { OR (c1, c2, c3, ...)
                          { OR (d1, d2, d3, ...)
                          { -- whatever the seed, regardless of how many

                               numbers are already generated

If you look at the result modulo 4 [rn2(4)] there will be only 8, etc.
Now that's stinky and obviously leads to problems.

